---
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
import { VOTING, VERIFIED } from "~/lib/voting";
import { formatDate, races } from "~/lib/data";

const munis = ["cnv", "dnv"] as const;
const allRaces = races();
---

<Base
  title="How to vote"
  description="Which North Vancouver municipality you vote in, when and where to vote, and what ID to bring for October 17, 2026."
>
  <h1>How to vote on October 17</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    North Vancouver is two separate municipalities with two separate ballots.
    Work out which one you're in first. Everything else follows from that.
  </p>

  <div class="notice">
    <p>
      <strong>Advance voting starts Wednesday, October 7</strong> in both
      municipalities. You do not have to wait for the 17th.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The names on the ballot are final</h2>
  <p>
    Candidates had until 4:00 p.m. on September 18 to withdraw their nomination.
    That deadline is set in provincial law and it passed weeks ago, so the list
    cannot change now. Every name on this site is a name you will see on your
    ballot paper.
  </p>
  <p>
    This matters because people do stop campaigning, and word gets around that
    someone has dropped out. Quietly giving up is not the same as withdrawing
    before the deadline. If you hear that a candidate is no longer running,
    check the
    <a href="https://www.cnv.org/City-Hall/General-Local-Election/2026-General-Local-Election/Candidate-Profiles" target="_blank" rel="noopener">City's own list</a>
    or the
    <a href="https://www.dnv.org/government-administration/see-who-is-running" target="_blank" rel="noopener">District's</a>
    rather than taking my word or anyone else's. Those two pages are the ballot.
  </p>

  <h2>Which one am I in?</h2>
  <p>
    They're genuinely different local governments, each with its own mayor and
    council. You vote in exactly one: whichever you live in, or if you own
    property here without living here, whichever the property is in.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>City of North Vancouver</strong> — the smaller, denser area around
      Lonsdale and the waterfront, roughly between the Capilano River and Lynn
      Creek south of about 29th Street.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>District of North Vancouver</strong> — the larger area wrapping
      around it: Lynn Valley, Deep Cove, Edgemont, Capilano Highlands, Seymour,
      Parkgate, Blueridge and the North Shore mountains.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Not sure? Your property tax or utility bill names the municipality that
    issued it. Or call either election office and they'll tell you in a minute.
    I deliberately don't guess this from your address, because sending you to
    the wrong ballot would be worse than asking you to check.
  </p>

  <h2>What to bring</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Two pieces of ID</strong>, at least one with your signature, together
    proving your name and address. A BC driver's licence, BC Services
    Card, passport, utility bill, property tax notice, credit or debit card, or
    student ID all count. If neither shows your address you can make a solemn
    declaration at the voting place. You don't need to register in advance;
    you can do it when you vote.
  </p>

  {munis.map((key) => {
    const m = VOTING[key];
    return (
      <section style="margin-top:36px">
        <div class="race-heading"><h2>{m.name}</h2></div>

        <h3 style="margin-top:18px">Advance voting</h3>
        <div class="grid grid-2">
          {m.advance.map((day) => (
            <div class="card">
              <strong>{day.date}</strong>
              <div class="candidate-meta">{day.hours}</div>
              <ul style="margin:8px 0 0; padding-left:18px; font-size:0.9rem">
                {day.places.map((p) => (
                  <li>{p.name}<br /><span style="color:var(--text-faint)">{p.address}</span></li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <h3 style="margin-top:22px">General voting day — Saturday, October 17</h3>
        <p class="candidate-meta">Open {m.generalDay.hours}. You may vote at any of these places.</p>
        <ul style="columns:2; column-gap:30px; font-size:0.92rem; padding-left:18px">
          {m.generalDay.places.map((p) => (
            <li style="break-inside:avoid; margin-bottom:5px">
              {p.name}<br />
              <span style="color:var(--text-faint)">{p.address}</span>
              {p.note === "curbside" && <span class="tag" style="margin-left:5px">curbside</span>}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>

        {m.special && <p class="candidate-meta">{m.special}</p>}
        {m.extras.map((e) => <p class="candidate-meta">{e}</p>)}

        <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--text-faint)">
          Source: <a href={m.source} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{m.name} election page</a> ·
          {" "}Elections office {m.contact.phone},
          {" "}<a href={`mailto:${m.contact.email}`}>{m.contact.email}</a>
        </p>
      </section>
    );
  })}

  <h2 style="margin-top:38px">How the ballot actually works</h2>
  <p>
    This trips up almost everyone, so plainly: <strong>you vote for as many
    people as there are seats, not one.</strong> Council elects six, so you may
    mark up to six names. Not one, and not a ranking. Every name you mark counts
    as a full vote for that person, and the six with the most votes win.
  </p>
  <p>
    You can mark fewer than six if you only want to support two or three. That's
    completely valid, and some people do it deliberately: every extra name you
    mark is a vote helping someone beat the candidates you actually want.
  </p>
  <div class="notice">
    <p>
      <strong>Don't mark more than the number of seats.</strong> Mark seven names
      for council and that part of your ballot can't be counted. Your mayor and
      school trustee choices are a separate contest on the same ballot and still
      count, but the council votes are lost. If you're unsure at the voting place,
      ask — that's what the election officials are there for.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p class="candidate-meta">
    If you'd like to work out your picks before you go, the
    <a href="/shortlist/">ballot list</a> counts them for you and warns you if
    you've starred too many in a race.
  </p>

  <h2 style="margin-top:38px">What's on your ballot</h2>
  <ul class="linklist">
    {allRaces.map((r) => (
      <li>
        <a class="tag" href={`/candidates/#${r.key}`} style="text-decoration:none">
          {r.label} — choose {r.seats} of {r.candidates.length}
        </a>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>
  <p class="candidate-meta" style="margin-top:10px">
    School trustees for School District 44 are elected by voters in both
    municipalities, but on separate ballots with different candidates.
  </p>

  <div class="notice" style="margin-top:28px">
    <p>
      I transcribed these dates, hours and locations from the two municipal
      websites and last checked them on {formatDate(VERIFIED)}. They're the ones
      that matter on the day, so if anything here disagrees with the official
      page, believe the official page and{" "}
      <a href="/methodology/#corrections">tell me</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
